# tier: original-diagnostic
# oracle: independent-derivation
# scenario: diag-stic2-border-pass
# profile: intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1
# frame: 5 F-M4
# program: fixtures/FX-diag-stic-2/diag-stic-2.listing.txt (ivs-diag-stic-2), entered through the
#   project boot stub contract 2 (boot-stub-2: cartridge reset entry 5000, interrupt entry 5004), with
#   the zero graphics ROM and no EXEC image
#
# Predicate of ivs-diag-stic-2 with KEY_2 closed from tick 0, the selector byte BE (plan 08-07, D-04, D-05,
#   D-10): the guest words at the one bound (marker 03C5, the frame counter 000A, the selector word 00BE, the
#   scene number 0004), frame 0 not displayed, frame 5 displayed, the picture F-M4 in frame 5 by hash (both
#   border-extension strips painted over the cards and over the MOB that lies under the corner, delay 0) and
#   four pixel probes, and ten delivered frames, all derived by hand in docs/video/diag-stic-2-derivation.md
#   (sections (a) to (f); the frame literals data/video/frames/F-M*.frame with their sha256-text keys) BEFORE
#   the program, its listing, the scenario or any run existed (oracle commit
#   641a12e3a1059b242cc88a3ee4308e3f58d9b36d, blind second read closed in commit 9250265), and copied here line
#   for line from the committed block. It was not generated by, read back from or edited to match the core or a
#   run. The author of this derivation also wrote the literals, the STIC rules, the program and the core, and
#   the blind reader is of the same model family, so all of them can share one misreading of a source; agreement
#   among them is not independent evidence about hardware.
# The scene does not change after frame 1 (the routine writes the same registers in every frame), so frame 5 is
#   four frames from the first routine and no frame assert names frame 0 or frame 1. F-M4 is derived under the
#   defaults of the pinned items PD-8 (a top strip of 8 plane rows) and PD-9 (strips fixed to the display); its
#   four pixel probes lie where neither pin changes the value (a strip position under a top strip of 8 rows and
#   of 4 rows alike, or a position that no strip covers, and the delay is 0 so a strip that follows the delay
#   would not move).
# Excluded on purpose: machine time (printed in the report, never asserted), the stack word [0340] (C68),
# all CPU registers, R7 and the flags (C40, C45, C56), and every pinned item of the derivation, section
# (i): the pixels of a MOB with the priority bit set (PD-1), half-height pixels (PD-2), a collision of a MOB
# at X coordinate 0 (PD-3), the border bit of a collision register (PD-4), a collision outside the plane
# (PD-5), a collision in a frame with the display off (PD-6), the read and clear semantics of the collision
# registers beyond sticky bits and a write of zero (PD-7), the height unit of the top strip (PD-8), a strip
# against the delay (PD-9), the content of the area a delay exposes and a MOB in it (PD-10, PD-11), the delay
# range (PD-12), and the bus windows against the vertical delay (PD-13).
# Body: one assert per clause in the runner's scenario grammar, hex upper case, exact compare, evaluated
# at the end of the step above it, in the order of data/scenarios/diag-stic2-border-pass.ivss
# (golden-expect-drift). The step line below is a comment.
# step: run-to 640000 ticks
assert halted == no
assert mem 02F0 == 03C5
assert mem 02F1 == 000A
assert mem 02F2 == 00BE
assert mem 02F3 == 0004
assert frame 0 displayed == no
assert frame 5 displayed == yes
assert frame 5 hash == 49b089730cf6c3b17e52f9b6aea588a258c9c5a8c28139ff5e4045dc5f4f5fa2
assert frame 5 pixel 0 0 == 8
assert frame 5 pixel 100 3 == 8
assert frame 5 pixel 8 8 == 4
assert frame 5 pixel 8 12 == 1
assert video count == 10
